  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'PAPER_EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid evolution of code generation agents powered by large language models (LLMs) has transformed software development workflows, enabling dynamic execution, debugging, and interactive programming capabilities. However, these advancements have introduced significant security concerns, as vulnerable code agents may be exploited to generate malicious code or execute risky operations. This paper reviews recent research advances in code agents and red-teaming methodologies designed to evaluate and improve their trustworthiness. We examine the landscape of code generation models, the emergence of automated red-teaming techniques, and the growing importance of blue-teaming approaches for defense. We analyze key contributions including DecodingTrust [1], which provides a comprehensive assessment of trustworthiness in GPT models, and BlueCodeAgent [2], which introduces automated red-teaming frameworks specifically for code generation AI systems. Through this survey, we identify critical gaps in current evaluation methodologies and propose directions for future research in securing code agents against adversarial attacks.\n\n## Introduction\n\nCode agents represent a paradigm shift in how developers interact with programming tools. By combining large language models with code interpreters, these agents can generate, execute, debug, and optimize code autonomously. This capability has led to widespread adoption in software engineering workflows, from automated testing to code review and refactoring. However, the integration of LLMs with execution environments introduces unique security challenges that traditional software security measures are ill-equipped to handle.\n\nThe primary concern lies in the dual nature of code agents: they must be trusted to generate correct code while being protected against attempts to misuse them. Adversarial actors may exploit vulnerabilities in code agents by crafting malicious prompts designed to elicit harmful code generation, bypass security filters, or execute unauthorized operations on connected systems. The consequences of such exploits can range from data exfiltration to complete system compromise.\n\nRecent research has begun to address these challenges through the lens of trustworthiness evaluation and automated red-teaming. The trustworthiness of AI systems encompasses multiple dimensions including safety, fairness, robustness, and privacy. For code agents, safety and robustness are particularly critical given the potentially destructive capabilities of generated code. Red-teaming, which involves simulating adversarial attacks to identify vulnerabilities, has emerged as a crucial methodology for evaluating and improving the security of these systems.\n\nThis paper surveys recent advances in code agent security, focusing on red-teaming and trustworthiness evaluation. We organize our discussion around key research contributions and emerging trends in the field. Section 2 reviews related work on trustworthiness assessment and automated red-teaming for code agents. Section 3 discusses methodological approaches and evaluation frameworks. Section 4 identifies current limitations and challenges. Section 5 outlines promising directions for future research. Finally, Section 6 concludes the paper with a summary of key findings.\n\n## Related Work\n\nThe trustworthiness of large language models has become an active area of research. DecodingTrust [1] provides one of the most comprehensive assessments of trustworthiness in GPT models, evaluating their safety across multiple dimensions. The authors conducted a large-scale evaluation of GPT models on their ability to generate harmful content, covering categories including bias, toxicity, privacy violations, and dangerous activities. Their findings revealed significant vulnerabilities in current models, with GPT-4 demonstrating improved safety compared to earlier versions but still generating harmful outputs in certain contexts. Importantly, DecodingTrust established systematic evaluation frameworks that have influenced subsequent research on AI safety, including work specifically focused on code generation models.\n\nThe emergence of code-specific agents has necessitated specialized trustworthiness evaluations. BlueCodeAgent [2] addresses this need by introducing a comprehensive framework for automated red-teaming of code generation AI. The authors identified a critical gap in existing research: while numerous studies have examined red-teaming for natural language generation, fewer have focused on the unique challenges posed by code generation. Code agents present distinct attack surfaces, including injection attacks through code comments, prompt injection targeting code interpreters, and exploitation of execution environments. BlueCodeAgent proposes a systematic approach to identifying these vulnerabilities through automated red-teaming, demonstrating that existing code generation models remain susceptible to adversarial attacks.\n\nThe relationship between red-teaming and blue-teaming in the context of code agents is complementary. Red-teaming focuses on identifying vulnerabilities by simulating adversarial attacks, while blue-teaming emphasizes defensive strategies for protecting systems. BlueCodeAgent [2] bridges these approaches by not only identifying vulnerabilities through red-teaming but also developing blue-teaming capabilities that leverage the insights gained from red-team exercises. This dual approach enables more robust defense mechanisms that are informed by a thorough understanding of attack strategies.\n\nOther relevant work includes RedCode [3], which introduces a benchmark for evaluating the security of code agents across diverse execution environments. The authors demonstrate that current code agents exhibit varying levels of vulnerability depending on their architecture and the